<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paku Alam VIII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1998">1998</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paku Alam VIII was the ruler of the Pakualaman principality in Yogyakarta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, from 1937 to 1998. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix">Hamengkubuwono IX</a> he supported the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> during the revolution against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. After Yogyakarta became a Special Region he served as its vice-governor and, after the sultan's death in 1988, as acting governor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paku-alam-viii.jpg" alt="Paku Alam VIII" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potret_KGPAA_Paku_Alam_VIII_saat_menjabat_sebagai_Gubernur_Daerah_Istimewa_Yogyakarta..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born on 10 April 1910, the son of Paku Alam VII and Gusti Bendara Raden Ayu Retno Poewoso, with the childhood name Gusti Raden Mas Harya Sularso Kunto Suratno. The Pakualaman, dating from 1812, is an enclave within the Yogyakarta Sultanate. He came to its throne on 12 April 1937 under the official name Kanjeng Gusti Pangeran Adipati Arya Paku Alam VIII.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 19 August 1945 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix">Hamengkubuwono IX</a> congratulated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a> on the founding of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and after the sultan declared his readiness to join the republic he did the same. On 5 September he decreed that the Pakualaman was an integral part of Indonesia, and by a joint declaration of 30 October the two rulers united their realms as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-region-of-yogyakarta">Special Region of Yogyakarta</a>, with the sultan and the prince serving for life as governor and vice-governor.</p><p>From 1946 to 1978 he often acted as governor while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix">Hamengkubuwono IX</a> held posts in the republic's cabinet and served as vice president. He led the elections commission in Yogyakarta for the local elections of 1951, 1955 and 1957, was elected to the Constitutional Assembly for the League of Supporters of Indonesian Independence (IPKI), and sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-consultative-assembly">People's Consultative Assembly</a> from 1997. On 20 May 1998, in response to the riots of that month, he and Hamengkubuwono X issued a joint declaration supporting a peaceful Reformasi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in office on 11 September 1998 and was buried in the family graveyard at Girigondo, having served longer than anyone as an Indonesian vice-governor and as prince of Pakualaman. He was credited with helping to found several universities, among them Gadjah Mada University and Yogyakarta State University, and in 1979 the Notokusumo Foundation, which opened academies of state administration and nursing. On 7 November 2022 he was named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paku Alam VIII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paku Alam VIII</b> supported the Republic of Indonesia, and when Yogyakarta became a Special Region in 1950 its rulers became vice-governors alongside the sultan, an arrangement confirmed in 2012.</blockquote><a href="/state/pakualaman">Pakualaman</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yogyakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in Yogyakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.8,110.39&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Yogyakarta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7603 2455 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paku Alam VIII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8053 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8053 2725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.8,110.39&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
